          "source_quote": "αὐτοῖς ὀργισθέντες ἐπιπλήξητε",
          "english_quote": "become angry with yourselves and rebuke yourselves",
          "issue": "The printed αὐτοῖς supports 'them', whereas the draft makes both anger and rebuke self-directed. The supplied excerpt does not establish this reflexive interpretation, and the notes do not disclose or justify it. This is a referent problem, not evidence of an OCR error.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use 'become angry with them and rebuke them', retaining the anaphoric pronoun without inventing an antecedent. If a reflexive rendering is proposed instead, provide a source-based grammatical and contextual justification, explicitly distinguishing the printed αὐτοῖς from the reflexive spelling αὑτοῖς, and preserve 'them' as an alternative.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ἵνα τὴν δικαίαν ὑμῶν ὀργὴν τῷ νῷ ὡς πῦρ χερσευσάσῃ ἀρούρῃ ἐπαφεῖναι δυνηθῆτε.",
          "english_quote": "Then you will be able to apply your righteous anger to your mind like fire to a field left uncultivated.",
          "issue": "The independent 'Then you will' presents a subsequent assured result rather than explicitly preserving the purpose or intended-result relationship introduced by ἵνα.",
          "suggested_revision": "Connect this to the preceding action with 'so that you may be able to apply your righteous anger to your mind like fire to a field left uncultivated.'",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "11.8",
