    {
      "reference": "11.13",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": true,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "οὐκ ὀφείλει ὁ υἱὸς νήψας τὸ κρεῖττον ἑλέσθαι, ἵνα μὴ ἀσεβήσῃ καὶ μὴ τὰ ἴσα τοῖς γονεῦσιν ποιῶν ἀμαρτάνῃ;",
          "english_quote": "is the son, once he has become clear-minded, not to choose the better course, lest he act impiously and sin by not doing the same things as his parents?",
          "issue": "The draft gives a defensible reductio reading, but its note does not disclose the full alternative involving both the rhetorical force of οὐκ ὀφείλει and the attachment of the second μή. That μή can negate the clause ending in ἀμαρτάνῃ rather than only the participle ποιῶν. On that reading, the son should choose better so that he does not sin by repeating his parents' conduct. This materially differs from sinning by failing to repeat it.",
          "suggested_revision": "Expand the note to preserve the full alternative: 'Is the son, once clear-minded, not obliged to choose the better course, so that he does not act impiously and does not sin by doing the same things as his parents?' Explain that the main text instead reads the question as a reductio of ancestral obligation and attaches the second μή to ποιῶν. This interpretive ambiguity is not itself a source-corruption block.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
